| `/test/` | Troy Goode, 517 of 966 lines (53.5%) | Troy holds half, but only just. Douglas Christopher Wilson holds 374 lines (38.7%). |
| `/.github/` | Nobody holds half | The top author is Douglas Christopher Wilson with 118 of 366 lines (32.2%). Íñigo Marquínez Prado has 95 (26.0%). No owner line. |
| Root files | Nobody holds half | The tool skips them, because a root pattern would also match every directory. The top author is Troy Goode with 134 of 415 lines (32.3%), then Douglas Christopher Wilson with 115 (27.7%). No owner line. |

**Merged identities.** Both `/lib/` and `/test/` depend on counting Troy Goode's two addresses as one person: `<email redacted>` and `troy@laptop.example`.
- This merge is a guess from matching names. I did not write it to `.mailmap`, so it is only in memory and Troy's identity is not confirmed.
- Unmerged, `/lib/` and `/test/` are not checked, but the tool shows `<email redacted>` alone has 762 lines across the repository and `troy@laptop.example` has 48. In `/test/`, the 53.5% may drop below half without the laptop address.
- I used the gmail address in the lines above because the tool printed it.
- A second suspected group, `alex94puchades` and Alejandro Caravaca Puchades, is not merged in these tables. Merging it would not change any owner, because their combined share is small.

**Left out of the counts:**
- Commit `62628c0` ("style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes", Fiona Formatter) is ignored. It is a whitespace and quote reformat that would otherwise take 291 lines (14.7%).
- One bot identity is excluded: 10 lines and 11 commits.
- The run covered all 18 files and attributed 1,985 of 1,995 lines. It was not a sample.

**Bus factor.** This is the smallest number of authors whose surviving lines together reach 50% of the lines in that scope. The tool printed only the 50% figure per directory, not the 80% figure.
- `/lib/`: 1 (Troy Goode, 66.8%).
- `/test/`: 1 (Troy Goode, 53.5%).
- Root files: 2 (Troy Goode and Douglas Christopher Wilson, 60.0%).
- `/.github/`: 2 (Douglas Christopher Wilson and Íñigo Marquínez Prado, 58.2%).

**What the numbers cannot show:** these figures count surviving lines, not code quality, review work or design work, and they ignore code that was deleted on purpose. They show who wrote the code that is left, not who should review changes to it.

